    "message": "Detect the commit actor instead of defaulting to human\n\nThe commit envelope exists to record whether a human, an agent, or CI wrote a\nchange. build_commit_envelope() read actor_type from .git/matrixscroll/config.json,\nwhich hook-install writes once with human and git-cli and never revisits, so\nagent commits in a hook-installed clone were signed as human authorship. The\nnotes ref in SSX360/digital-rain carries three envelopes reading actor_type\nhuman with tool cursor-agent, which is the defect stated outright.\n\nbuild_commit_envelope() now inspects the environment first and only falls back\nto the config file. Agent markers are checked before CI markers so an agent\ndriving a commit inside a pipeline is still recorded as an agent, and detection\noutranks the config file because a file written at install time must not be\nable to claim human authorship for a machine. Detection can only move\nattribution away from human, so a false positive costs the accuracy of a tool\nname and can never manufacture a human record.\n\nHistorical notes are left alone. Re-signing an envelope in place would produce\na validly signed false record, schema v1 defines no superseding record, and\nverification reads a note with one json.loads() over the whole blob so an\nappended correcting note would fail to parse. The reasoning is written up in\ndocs/explanation/actor-attribution.md.\n\nCo-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>\n",
